\vspace{1em}

\begin{abstract}
Brain tumor classification from magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) is mostly reported using a single train/test split and a single backbone network. This kind of evaluation does not show how much the reported number depends on which images fall into the test set. It also does not show whether the same result holds for a different network. Our main target is to evaluate a hybrid pipeline in a way that answers both of these questions. The pipeline combines two-stage backbone fine-tuning, class-weighted training, test-time augmentation (TTA) and a soft-voting ensemble of Random Forest, XGBoost and Support Vector Machine (SVM) trained on deep embeddings. We run this pipeline under 5-fold cross-validation on four backbones: EfficientNetB0, ResNet50, DenseNet121 and MobileNetV2. All four backbones are trained and tested with the same protocol on a four-class dataset of 7,200 MRI images containing glioma, meningioma, pituitary tumor and no-tumor cases. ResNet50 with TTA gives the highest mean accuracy, $98.38\% \pm 0.23\%$. DenseNet121 is close to it with $98.32\% \pm 0.22\%$. The DenseNet121 embedding ensemble gives $98.00\% \pm 0.34\%$. MobileNetV2 gives the best trade-off between cost and accuracy. It trains about 14\% faster than EfficientNetB0 and 30\% faster than ResNet50, it has the lowest inference latency, and it still reaches $97.96\% \pm 0.32\%$ with TTA. An exact Wilcoxon signed-rank test on the five paired folds shows that ResNet50 and DenseNet121 are significantly better than EfficientNetB0 ($p = 0.031$ for both). The gain of MobileNetV2 is not significant ($p = 0.156$). TTA improves accuracy significantly for all four backbones ($p = 0.031$). Grad-CAM maps show that the models look at anatomically relevant regions and not at image artifacts. These results show that multi-backbone and cross-validated evaluation is important for MRI based brain tumor classification, and that it gives a more reliable picture of both accuracy and computational cost.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} Brain Tumor Classification, Convolutional Neural Network, Transfer Learning, Backbone Comparison, Ensemble Learning, Cross-Validation, Statistical Significance Testing, Magnetic Resonance Imaging

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Brain tumors occur when abnormal cells grow uncontrollably inside the brain \cite{dorfner2025review}. Depending on where the tumor forms, it can affect motor function, memory or basic cognition. The WHO classification recognizes dozens of tumor types and grades \cite{louis2021who}. But for imaging based triage, the coarser grouping used in this paper is still the basis of most automated systems. These four groups are glioma, meningioma, pituitary tumor and no tumor. Early and accurate diagnosis is important because treatment planning depends on it \cite{dorfner2025review}. The scale of the problem is large. In the United States alone, the American Cancer Society estimates around 24,740 new malignant brain and CNS tumor diagnoses for 2026, with roughly 18,350 deaths \cite{acs2026braintumor}.

Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) is the most common imaging method for this task. It shows soft tissue detail well and it does not require any invasive procedure \cite{dorfner2025review}. Still a radiologist has to review the scan and make the diagnosis. This takes time and the reading can vary from one reader to another. Automated tools try to close this gap by making the process faster and more consistent. They are not meant to replace the radiologist \cite{karagoz2024resvit}.

Deep learning, especially CNNs, has improved this problem a lot in recent years \cite{dorfner2025review, kaifi2024enhancing}. But medical imaging datasets are usually small compared to what these models need, so overfitting is a common issue \cite{karagoz2024resvit}. One common solution is to combine a CNN with a traditional classifier. Here the network works as a feature extractor and a classifier such as Random Forest takes the final decision \cite{alzoghby2023dual}. Data augmentation is another common technique used to get more out of a limited dataset.

Two evaluation issues in this literature motivate our work. The first one is the data split. Many papers report accuracy from only one train/test split, and this number can change a lot depending on which images end up in the test set. Cross-validation uses multiple splits and averages the results, so it gives a more reliable estimate. We use cross-validation throughout this paper. The second issue is the backbone, and it is discussed less often. Most papers in this area report results for a single backbone network. So it stays unclear whether a reported accuracy comes from the pipeline itself, that is the fine-tuning strategy, class weighting, TTA and ensembling, or only from the specific network that was chosen.

In this paper we treat both issues as experiments and not as footnotes. The contributions of this paper are as follows:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item We evaluate a hybrid pipeline of two-stage fine-tuning, TTA and embedding ensemble under 5-fold stratified cross-validation on a four-class, 7,200-image brain tumor MRI dataset. We report mean and standard deviation accuracy instead of a single train/test split number.
    \item We repeat the same evaluation on four backbones (EfficientNetB0, ResNet50, DenseNet121, MobileNetV2) under an identical protocol. This shows whether the reported gains are pipeline level or backbone specific, and it also gives the accuracy, latency and training cost trade-off across backbones.
    \item We check the gains from TTA, the embedding ensemble and the stronger backbones with an exact Wilcoxon signed-rank test on the fold-wise accuracy scores, instead of comparing mean accuracy only.
    \item We use Grad-CAM to see whether the predictions of the network are anchored to anatomically relevant regions of the MRI slice and not to image artifacts.
\end{enumerate}


\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

CNNs are widely used for brain tumor classification because they can extract hierarchical features from an MRI slice without manual feature engineering. Dorfner et al. \cite{dorfner2025review} were among the early groups to show that this works well for both segmentation and classification, with the aim of reducing the subjectivity of manual review. Kaifi \cite{kaifi2024enhancing} used a modified softsign activation function and reported 97.6\% accuracy on the same four-class glioma/meningioma/pituitary/no-tumor problem that we use here. Both works show CNNs are useful for this task. But they also face two common limitations, which are limited interpretability and a constant need for more labeled data than these datasets provide.

A common solution to the labeled-data problem is to combine CNNs with traditional classifiers instead of using the output of the network only. Al-Zoghby et al. \cite{alzoghby2023dual} used a dual-CNN classifier published in \emph{Diagnostics} and reported 99\% test accuracy. Vision Transformers \cite{dosovitskiy2021vit} have also become a popular alternative, mainly because they can model long-range spatial relationships that convolutions may miss. Karagoz et al. \cite{karagoz2024resvit} combined a Residual Vision Transformer with self-supervised pretraining to handle the limited label problem. This is a reasonable choice because most public brain tumor datasets are small. G\'omez-Guzm\'an et al. \cite{gomezguzman2025swin} benchmarked several pretrained CNN and transformer backbones on the same Kaggle dataset identifier that we use. Their release has 7,023 images, while the release we use has 7,200 images and is exactly class balanced (Section~\ref{sec:limitations}). Their Swin-Tiny transformer reached 99.24\% on a single 75/25 split. So transformer backbones are now competitive with CNNs on this problem. But like most results in this comparison, the number comes from a single split and from a single backbone, not from cross-validation and not from a systematic comparison.

Data augmentation techniques are also common in this area. Standard techniques such as rotation and flipping are used to expand small datasets, and GAN based synthesis \cite{goodfellow2014gan} is used more and more for the same purpose. Self-supervised pretraining on unlabeled scans has also become popular. Transfer learning from ImageNet-scale pretraining transfers well to medical imaging in spite of the domain gap \cite{morid2021transfer}.

Two limitations are common across this literature. First, interpretability is often not addressed directly. Grad-CAM and similar attention based explanations are usually mentioned as future work and not actually reported. Second, a large number of these studies report accuracy from a single train/test split instead of cross-validation. So it is hard to know how much of the reported accuracy is real performance and how much is split-specific variance. This is a broader methodological issue in medical imaging machine learning in general and not only in this subfield \cite{varoquaux2022leakage}. Again, almost none of these studies report results for more than one backbone under an identical training and evaluation protocol. So it is generally unclear how much of a reported result would transfer to a different but equally reasonable network.

Table~\ref{tab:related_work} summarizes the studies discussed above together with the evaluation protocol each one used. Except G\'omez-Guzm\'an et al. \cite{gomezguzman2025swin}, none of these studies report a cross-validated accuracy, and none of them compare more than one backbone under a controlled protocol. These are the two gaps that Section~\ref{sec:methodology} addresses.

