\section{Methodology}
\label{sec:methodology}

In this work we build a machine learning framework for multi-class brain tumor classification around two linked experiments. The first one is a multi-backbone, cross-validated comparison of the hybrid pipeline. The second one is the statistical validation of the gains we get. Figure~\ref{fig:framework} shows the complete framework. The rest of this section describes each stage one by one.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\textwidth]{images/methodology_without_emoji.png}
\caption{Overview of the proposed hybrid CNN--ensemble framework. From top to bottom: the input four-class MRI dataset; preprocessing (resize and normalize, augmentation, stratified 5-fold splitting, and class balancing); feature engineering via transfer learning; the hybrid model architecture, combining two-stage fine-tuned backbones (EfficientNetB0, ResNet50, DenseNet121, MobileNetV2) with a soft-voting Random Forest/XGBoost/SVM ensemble on the extracted embeddings; training and optimization; statistically validated evaluation (accuracy, precision, recall, F1, and the Wilcoxon signed-rank test) with Grad-CAM visualization; and the final four-class prediction.}
\label{fig:framework}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Dataset Description}
\label{sec:datasets}

For our work we use a four-class brain tumor MRI dataset of 7,200 images. It has 1,800 images each of glioma, meningioma, pituitary tumor and no tumor. We checked this directly (Section~\ref{sec:eda}) and the dataset is exactly class balanced. Some earlier works that use the same Kaggle dataset identifier report a total of 7,023 images with an imbalanced and smaller no-tumor class. This most likely comes from an earlier release of the dataset and not from the snapshot we use. Section~\ref{sec:limitations} discusses what this means for the benchmarking comparison in Section~\ref{sec:sota}. Table~\ref{tab:datasets} shows the operational characteristics of the dataset we actually use.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Operational characteristics of the dataset used in this study.}
\label{tab:datasets}
\small
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{@{}>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{4.2cm}>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}X@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Property} & \textbf{Value} \\
\midrule
Classes & Glioma, meningioma, pituitary tumor, no-tumor (4) \\
Samples & 7,200 images (1,800 per class) \\
Patients recorded & No \\
Split used & Image-level, stratified 5-fold \\
Backbones evaluated & EfficientNetB0, ResNet50, DenseNet121, MobileNetV2 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{table}

The dataset comes as separate Training and Testing folders. It does not record a patient or subject identifier for any image. We merge both folders and re-split the combined data using stratified 5-fold cross-validation, so every image is used as test data exactly once. As no patient identifier is available, this split is done at the image level. Section~\ref{sec:limitations} discusses what this means for the accuracy we report.

