\subsection{Exploratory Data Analysis}
\label{sec:eda}

Before training any model we check the dataset directly instead of assuming that it matches the way it is usually described in earlier work. We look at class balance, image dimensions, per-class pixel intensity and a visual sample of each class. Figure~\ref{fig:eda_class} shows that the class distribution is exactly balanced at 1,800 images per class. This is already different from the imbalanced count reported for this dataset in some earlier work (Section~\ref{sec:limitations}). Figure~\ref{fig:eda_dims} shows that the image dimensions are not uniform across the merged dataset. There is a bimodal cluster around 220 and 512 pixels and a long tail up to 1,446 pixels. For this reason every image is explicitly resized to 224$\times$224 during preprocessing (Section~\ref{sec:preprocessing}) and not assumed to be the right size already.

Figure~\ref{fig:eda_samples} shows five representative images per class. It makes two things visible that the summary statistics do not show. First, the images inside one class are not all from the same anatomical plane. Axial, coronal and sagittal views are mixed together inside every class. Second, at least one no-tumor example is clearly a CT scan and not an MRI slice. It has a bright skull ring against a dark interior, which is not the T1-weighted MRI contrast seen in the other images. We mention this here and not only in Section~\ref{sec:limitations}, because it directly qualifies the ``T1-weighted'' description used for the whole dataset.

Figure~\ref{fig:eda_intensity} shows the distribution of mean pixel intensity per class. We expected no clear difference here, but no-tumor slices are visibly and consistently brighter than the three tumor classes. They have a higher median intensity and a tighter spread. So this is a real and systematic difference between no-tumor and the tumor classes. It is also a plausible reason why no-tumor is consistently the easiest class to classify correctly (Section~\ref{sec:backbone_results}). Figure~\ref{fig:eda_mean} shows the pixel-wise average image per class. The no-tumor average is visibly sharper and less blurred than the tumor-class averages, which fits with more consistent framing and alignment in the no-tumor slices. The tumor classes show more positional variation. This is expected, because the location of a tumor varies from patient to patient while a healthy slice is more likely to be taken from a standard anatomical position.

