\label{fig:eda_mean}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Near-duplicate audit.} Section~\ref{sec:limitations} raises subject-level leakage as a concern for this dataset, because it has no patient identifier and it is itself a merge of several public sources. We tried to test this directly with a 64-bit average-hash Hamming-distance scan. Each image is downsampled to an 8$\times$8 grayscale thumbnail and thresholded against its own mean. This first attempt failed a basic sanity check, so we discarded it instead of reporting it. Even at the strictest possible threshold, that is Hamming distance 0 or an exact hash match, only 49\% of the flagged pairs shared the same class label. The base rate expected from four balanced classes with no signal at all is 25\%, so the result is close to chance and far below what a real near-duplicate detector should give. Manual inspection showed the reason. At 8$\times$8 resolution the hash is dominated by the dark background and centered oval skull layout that all four classes share (Figure~\ref{fig:eda_mean}), and not by the class-specific content that separates a true near-duplicate pair from two unrelated slices. A corrected two-stage procedure is needed here. The coarse hash should be used only as a cheap candidate filter, and each candidate pair should then be verified by direct pixel-level comparison of the resized images. This revision is left as ongoing work (Section~\ref{sec:limitations}). We report the failure here instead of publishing the uncorrected numbers.

\subsection{Preprocessing}
\label{sec:preprocessing}
Each image is decoded, resized to 224$\times$224 and normalized with the preprocessing function that matches the original ImageNet training regime of each backbone (Section~\ref{sec:backbones}). A mismatched normalization silently degrades the accuracy of a backbone without raising any error. So we apply it per backbone and do not reuse the normalization of one backbone for all four. The dataset we use is exactly class balanced at 1,800 images per class (Section~\ref{sec:eda}), but we still apply class-weighted training instead of assuming balance beforehand. Class imbalance is common in this literature and other releases of this dataset are not balanced \cite{buda2018imbalance}. The weights are the inverse class frequency inside each training fold. We compute them with the \texttt{compute\_class\_weight} function of \texttt{sklearn} in \texttt{balanced} mode and apply them directly to the loss function. As the classes are balanced here, these weights are almost uniform and have very little practical effect on our results.

For augmentation we use a standard set of transformations. These are random rotation, translation, zoom, horizontal flip and contrast jitter, implemented in a Keras \texttt{Sequential} block. We use this block for two purposes. During training it works as a standard overfitting guard, and during inference the same block is reused for test-time augmentation (Section~\ref{sec:inference}).

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{images/Brain tumor classification.drawio.png}
\caption{Overview of the proposed pipeline: a CNN backbone (one of four compared in this study) with two-stage fine-tuning, followed by two complementary inference heads -- a softmax classifier with test-time augmentation, and a soft-voting ensemble (Random Forest, XGBoost, SVM) trained on the CNN's penultimate-layer embeddings.}
\label{fig:workflow}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Backbone Architectures Compared}
\label{sec:backbones}
Instead of reporting results for a single network, we compare four widely used ImageNet-pretrained CNN backbones under an identical training and evaluation protocol. In this way any accuracy difference comes from the backbone and not from a difference in how it was trained or evaluated. The four backbones are:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{EfficientNetB0} \cite{tan2019efficientnet}, the backbone of our original pipeline. It is chosen for its compound scaling of depth, width and resolution, which gives a good accuracy to parameter trade-off.
    \item \textbf{ResNet50} \cite{he2016resnet}, a widely used residual architecture. Its identity shortcut connections reduce the vanishing gradient problem in deep networks. We include it as a strong and well established baseline.
    \item \textbf{DenseNet121} \cite{huang2017densenet}, which connects each layer to every other layer in a feed-forward way and so encourages feature reuse. We include it to see whether denser connectivity helps on this task.
    \item \textbf{MobileNetV2} \cite{sandler2018mobilenetv2}, a lightweight architecture built on inverted residuals and linear bottlenecks. We include it to see the accuracy and latency trade-off at the low-compute end.
\end{itemize}

For every backbone we add an identical classification head on top of the backbone feature output. The head has global average pooling, batch normalization \cite{ioffe2015batchnorm}, dropout of 0.4 \cite{srivastava2014dropout}, a 128-unit dense layer that also works as the embedding for the ensemble of Section~\ref{sec:inference} (batch-normalized, dropout 0.3), and a final softmax layer. Only the backbone itself and its matching input normalization differ between the four configurations. The head architecture, optimizer, learning rates, epoch budgets, augmentation policy and cross-validation protocol are kept fixed (Section~\ref{sec:cv_protocol}). So the comparison in Section~\ref{sec:backbone_results} isolates the contribution of the backbone.

\subsection{Two-Stage Fine-Tuning}
\label{sec:finetuning}
Training is done in two stages for every backbone. In Stage 1 the backbone is completely frozen and only the new head is trained, using Adam \cite{kingma2015adam} with learning rate $10^{-3}$ for 8 epochs. This lets the randomly initialized head stabilize before the pretrained weights are modified. In Stage 2 the top 80\% of the backbone is unfrozen and the whole network is fine-tuned together at a much lower learning rate of $10^{-5}$ for up to 35 epochs. Here we use early stopping on validation loss with patience 10 and a reduce-on-plateau schedule with factor 0.5 and patience 4. For every backbone we tested, unfreezing causes a temporary drop in accuracy at the start of Stage 2. It recovers within a few epochs and ends higher than the plateau of Stage 1 alone.

\subsection{Dual Inference Strategy}
\label{sec:inference}
After the CNN is trained there are multiple ways to produce a final prediction. We evaluate two of them for every backbone.

\textbf{Test-time augmentation (TTA).} At test time each image is passed through the model once without augmentation and 5 more times with the same augmentations used during training. The resulting softmax outputs are averaged. This follows an approach used in the uncertainty estimation literature \cite{wang2019tta}. It adds no extra training cost, only a small increase in inference time, which we measure in Section~\ref{sec:efficiency}.

\textbf{CNN-embedding ensemble.} Here the 128-dimensional embedding is extracted from the penultimate layer of the CNN, that is before the softmax, for every image. Three classical models are then trained on this embedding. These are Random Forest \cite{breiman2001randomforests} with 300 trees and balanced class weights, XGBoost \cite{chen2016xgboost} with 300 estimators, depth 5 and learning rate 0.05, and an RBF SVM with balanced weights and probability outputs enabled. The three models are combined by soft voting. The idea is that the deep network already produces a useful representation of the input, and a small ensemble trained on this representation may add robustness over the softmax head of the network itself.

\subsection{Cross-Validation Protocol}
\label{sec:cv_protocol}
The Training and Testing folders are merged and split into 5 stratified folds, keeping class proportions consistent across the folds. Inside the training portion of each fold we use a further 85/15 stratified split for validation and early stopping. Every image appears in the test set exactly once across the five folds. As the dataset does not record a patient identifier, this stratification works at the image level. It guarantees balanced class proportions per fold, but it cannot guarantee that two slices from the same scan stay on the same side of a fold, because there is no field in the dataset that would let us group them. Section~\ref{sec:limitations} discusses this further. The same 5-fold split, with identical random seed and identical fold assignment, is reused for all four backbones. This is what makes the fold-wise comparison in Section~\ref{sec:stats_results} a fair paired comparison and not a comparison across different test sets.

We report the mean and standard deviation across the five folds and not the result of a single run. All training was done on Kaggle with a single Tesla T4 GPU and batch size 16. Wall-clock training and inference time were measured directly in code for every fold of all four backbones (Section~\ref{sec:efficiency}). As a reproducibility check we re-ran the EfficientNetB0 configuration in a separate Kaggle session, through the same timing-instrumented script used for the other three backbones. The 5-fold mean accuracy of the second run is 96.01\% for the CNN, 97.75\% for CNN+TTA and 97.26\% for the ensemble. The first run gives 96.07\%, 97.74\% and 97.36\%, with the identical fixed random seed and the identical code. Individual folds differ by up to roughly 0.4 percentage points. We attribute this to the well known non-determinism of some cuDNN convolution and reduction kernels on GPU, which a fixed Python, NumPy and TensorFlow random seed does not fully remove. Throughout this paper we report the second, timing-instrumented run, so that it stays consistent with the other three backbones. We note the run-to-run difference here as a small but real source of variance on top of the fold-to-fold variance already captured by the standard deviations in Table~\ref{tab:backbone_results}.

\subsection{Statistical Significance Testing}
\label{sec:stats_protocol}
A higher mean accuracy across folds is not by itself evidence that the difference is not due to chance, especially with only five folds. To check this we apply the Wilcoxon signed-rank test \cite{wilcoxon1945} to the fold-wise accuracy scores and treat each fold as a paired observation. For example, the accuracy of EfficientNetB0 on fold $k$ is paired with the accuracy of ResNet50 on the same fold $k$. This is valid because all four backbones share identical fold assignments, as noted in Section~\ref{sec:cv_protocol}. We use the Wilcoxon test and not a paired $t$-test, because it does not assume that the differences are normally distributed. With only five paired observations per comparison that is the more defensible assumption \cite{demsar2006statistical}. As $n=5$ is small, we compute the exact one-sided p-value by enumerating all $2^5=32$ possible sign assignments of the ranked differences instead of using the normal approximation. At this sample size the smallest attainable one-sided $p$-value is $1/32 \approx 0.031$, which is worth keeping in mind while reading the results in Section~\ref{sec:stats_results}. We run the test for CNN vs.\ CNN+TTA and CNN vs.\ the embedding ensemble for each of the four backbones, for EfficientNetB0 vs.\ each of the other three backbones, and for ResNet50 vs.\ DenseNet121.


\section{Results and Discussion}
\label{sec:results}

The evaluation follows the same order as the contributions listed in Section~\ref{sec:intro}. These are the multi-backbone comparison, statistical validation, computational efficiency, benchmarking against earlier work and interpretability.

\subsection{Multi-Backbone Comparison}
\label{sec:backbone_results}

Table~\ref{tab:backbone_results} reports the mean $\pm$ standard deviation accuracy across the 5 folds for each of the four backbones under each of the three inference strategies, that is CNN softmax, CNN+TTA and embedding ensemble. Figure~\ref{fig:backbone_bar} shows the same data as a grouped bar chart.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{5-fold cross-validation accuracy across four backbone architectures and three inference strategies.}
\label{tab:backbone_results}
\small
\begin{tabular*}{\textwidth}{@{\extracolsep{\fill}}lccc@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Backbone} & \textbf{CNN (softmax)} & \textbf{CNN + TTA} & \textbf{Embedding ensemble} \\
\midrule
EfficientNetB0 & 96.01\% $\pm$ 0.38\% & 97.75\% $\pm$ 0.25\% & 97.26\% $\pm$ 0.27\% \\
ResNet50       & 97.18\% $\pm$ 0.56\% & \textbf{98.38\%} $\pm$ 0.23\% & 97.64\% $\pm$ 0.41\% \\
DenseNet121    & \textbf{97.69\%} $\pm$ 0.53\% & 98.32\% $\pm$ \textbf{0.22\%} & \textbf{98.00\%} $\pm$ 0.34\% \\
MobileNetV2    & 95.04\% $\pm$ 0.72\% & 97.96\% $\pm$ 0.32\% & 96.25\% $\pm$ 0.23\% \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular*}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\textwidth]{images/backbone_accuracy_bar.png}
\caption{Mean $\pm$ standard deviation 5-fold accuracy for four backbones under three inference strategies. Error bars show one standard deviation across folds.}
\label{fig:backbone_bar}
\end{figure}

ResNet50 and DenseNet121 both outperform the original EfficientNetB0 backbone on every inference strategy. DenseNet121 also has the lowest fold-to-fold variance for CNN+TTA, 0.22\% against 0.25\% for EfficientNetB0, and it reaches the highest embedding-ensemble accuracy of 98.00\%. The raw CNN softmax accuracy of MobileNetV2 is 95.04\%, which is clearly the weakest of the four. But TTA closes most of this gap and brings it to 97.96\%. That is within 0.4 percentage points of the best backbone, although MobileNetV2 is the lightest network in the comparison (Section~\ref{sec:efficiency}). Figure~\ref{fig:backbone_variance} shows the per-fold spread for CNN+TTA directly. The fold-wise accuracies of ResNet50 and DenseNet121 sit in a narrower and higher band than those of EfficientNetB0, while the folds of MobileNetV2 are more spread out in spite of a competitive mean.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.7\textwidth]{images/backbone_fold_variance.png}
\caption{Per-fold CNN+TTA accuracy for each backbone. Horizontal bars mark the mean across the five folds.}
\label{fig:backbone_variance}
\end{figure}

Table~\ref{tab:perclass_resnet} shows the per-class results for the best fold we observed in this study, which is ResNet50 with CNN+TTA. Table~\ref{tab:perclass} shows the corresponding best fold for the original EfficientNetB0 backbone, for a direct comparison. In both cases all four classes, including no-tumor, give strong results. The pattern of which class is hardest is also the same across backbones, and that class is meningioma.

