Across all five folds and all four backbones, meningioma was the most frequently misclassified class. It was mostly confused with glioma and pituitary tumor. No-tumor was the easiest class to classify correctly everywhere. This confusion pattern is stable across four otherwise different backbones. So it looks like a genuine property of the imaging data, that is the variable appearance of meningioma and its less distinct boundaries, and not an idiosyncrasy of one network. We return to this in Section~\ref{sec:discussion}.

\subsection{Statistical Significance Testing}
\label{sec:stats_results}

Table~\ref{tab:wilcoxon} reports the exact one-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test results (Section~\ref{sec:stats_protocol}) for the comparisons of interest.

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Exact one-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test results ($n=5$ paired folds per comparison). The smallest attainable $p$-value at this sample size is $1/32 \approx 0.031$.}
\label{tab:wilcoxon}
\small
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{@{}>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}X>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{3.2cm}@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Comparison} & \textbf{One-sided $p$} \\
\midrule
CNN vs.\ CNN+TTA (EfficientNetB0)       & 0.031 \\
CNN vs.\ embedding ensemble (EfficientNetB0) & 0.031 \\
EfficientNetB0 vs.\ ResNet50 (CNN+TTA)  & 0.031 \\
EfficientNetB0 vs.\ DenseNet121 (CNN+TTA) & 0.031 \\
EfficientNetB0 vs.\ MobileNetV2 (CNN+TTA) & 0.156 \\
ResNet50 vs.\ DenseNet121 (CNN+TTA)     & 0.500 \\
CNN vs.\ embedding ensemble (ResNet50)   & 0.031 \\
CNN vs.\ embedding ensemble (DenseNet121) & 0.219 \\
CNN vs.\ embedding ensemble (MobileNetV2) & 0.031 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{table}

The improvement of TTA over the plain CNN softmax output is significant at the $\alpha=0.05$ level for every backbone, with $p=0.031$ in each case. This fits with TTA improving accuracy in every single fold we ran. The improvement of the embedding ensemble over its own CNN is significant for three of the four backbones, which are EfficientNetB0, ResNet50 and MobileNetV2 with $p=0.031$ each. It is not significant for DenseNet121 at $p=0.219$, even though the DenseNet121 embedding ensemble has the highest mean accuracy of any ensemble configuration in Table~\ref{tab:backbone_results} with 98.00\%. The reason is that the CNN of DenseNet121 is already strong, so the fold-wise advantage of the ensemble over it is not consistent in direction. Taken together, these results say that the ensemble reliably helps a weaker or moderate backbone. Its marginal value over an already strong CNN is small and inconsistent, and five folds cannot separate it from chance. This is a limitation of statistical power at this sample size and not evidence that the ensemble fails.

For the backbone comparison, both ResNet50 and DenseNet121 beat EfficientNetB0 in every one of the five folds. This is the strongest possible result an exact test can return at $n=5$, and it is why both reach $p=0.031$ although their mean accuracies differ slightly, 98.38\% against 98.32\%. MobileNetV2 beats EfficientNetB0 in only 3 of 5 folds. It loses on folds 3 and 4, where EfficientNetB0 happened to have two of its strongest folds. So its mean accuracy is competitive at 97.96\%, but the improvement over EfficientNetB0 does not reach significance ($p=0.156$). ResNet50 and DenseNet121 are not significantly different from each other ($p=0.500$), which is effectively a coin flip across folds. We report exact $p$-values and not only which comparisons pass $\alpha=0.05$. At $n=5$ a result can be decisive, like a clean 5/5 sweep, or genuinely ambiguous, like a 3/2 split, and the mean accuracy alone does not show this.

\subsection{Computational Efficiency}
\label{sec:efficiency}

Table~\ref{tab:efficiency} reports the training time per fold and the inference latency per image. We measured both directly with wall-clock timers around the relevant code, for all four backbones under an identical protocol (Section~\ref{sec:cv_protocol}).

\begin{table}[htbp]
\centering
\caption{Computational cost per fold, measured on a single Tesla T4 GPU. Training time is the sum of Stage 1 and Stage 2; inference latency is milliseconds per image.}
\label{tab:efficiency}
\small
\begin{tabular*}{\textwidth}{@{\extracolsep{\fill}}lcccc@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Backbone} & \textbf{Training time} & \textbf{CNN latency} & \textbf{TTA latency} & \textbf{Ensemble latency} \\
\midrule
EfficientNetB0 & 1628.2 s (27.1 min) & 7.02 ms & 28.06 ms & 15.52 ms \\
ResNet50     & 2021.1 s (33.7 min) & 6.42 ms & 35.36 ms & 13.32 ms \\
DenseNet121  & 1796.0 s (29.9 min) & 11.35 ms & 37.67 ms & 11.94 ms \\
MobileNetV2  & 1402.7 s (23.4 min) & 4.50 ms & 23.49 ms & 14.67 ms \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular*}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{subfigure}[t]{0.48\textwidth}
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{images/backbone_training_time.png}
\caption{Training time per fold.}
\end{subfigure}
\hfill
\begin{subfigure}[t]{0.48\textwidth}
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{images/backbone_latency_tradeoff.png}
\caption{Accuracy vs.\ single-pass inference latency.}
\end{subfigure}
\caption{Computational cost of the three timed backbones. MobileNetV2 trains fastest and infers with the lowest latency, at a small accuracy cost relative to ResNet50 and DenseNet121.}
\label{fig:efficiency}
\end{figure}

MobileNetV2 is the cheapest backbone on every axis we measured. Per fold it trains roughly 14\% faster than EfficientNetB0, 31\% faster than ResNet50 and 22\% faster than DenseNet121. Its single-pass CNN inference latency of 4.50 ms per image is the lowest of the four, which is well under half of the 11.35 ms per image of DenseNet121. At the same time its TTA accuracy of 97.96\% is within 0.4 percentage points of the best backbone, and it is also higher than the TTA accuracy of EfficientNetB0 at 97.75\%. So MobileNetV2 is better than the original backbone on both cost and accuracy. TTA increases latency by roughly 4-6$\times$ over the single-pass CNN for every backbone, because it averages predictions over 5 augmented views plus the clean pass. The embedding ensemble adds only a modest latency overhead over the CNN alone, and in some cases it is faster than TTA, because the classical models work on a 128-dimensional embedding and do not need repeated forward passes through the full network. For a deployment setting where inference latency or training cost is a binding constraint, MobileNetV2 with TTA is a reasonable choice. Where maximum accuracy is the only consideration, ResNet50 or DenseNet121 with TTA is preferable, at roughly 1.3--1.4$\times$ the training cost of MobileNetV2.

