Our best configuration is ResNet50+TTA at 98.38\% $\pm$ 0.23\%. It is competitive with the strongest single-split numbers in the table, which are Res-BRNet at 98.22\% and ResViT+SSL at 98.47\%. Only the Swin-Tiny transformer at 99.24\% is higher. But unlike every other row in the table, our numbers come with an error estimate and they were obtained across four independent backbone architectures and not one. We consider this a more meaningful basis for comparison, even where our mean is below the single best reported figure.

\subsection{Interpretability: Grad-CAM Visualization}
\label{sec:gradcam}
High accuracy alone does not show whether the model is really focusing on the tumor or on some unrelated pattern in the image. To check this we use Gradient-weighted Class Activation Mapping (Grad-CAM) \cite{selvaraju2017gradcam} on the last convolutional feature map of the trained EfficientNetB0 network. Fig.~\ref{fig:gradcam} shows one correctly classified example per class, with the heatmap overlaid on the original slice.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{subfigure}[t]{0.48\linewidth}
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{images/gradcam_glioma_0_correct.png}
\caption{Glioma}
\end{subfigure}
\begin{subfigure}[t]{0.48\linewidth}
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{images/gradcam_meningioma_0_correct.png}
\caption{Meningioma}
\end{subfigure}

\begin{subfigure}[t]{0.48\linewidth}
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{images/gradcam_notumor_0_correct.png}
\caption{No Tumor}
\end{subfigure}
\begin{subfigure}[t]{0.48\linewidth}
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{images/gradcam_pituitary_0_correct.png}
\caption{Pituitary Tumor}
\end{subfigure}
\caption{Grad-CAM visualizations (original, heatmap, overlay) for one correctly classified example per class. The model's attention (red regions) localizes to the visible mass for glioma and meningioma, to the sellar/skull-base region where the pituitary gland is located for pituitary tumor, and shows a diffuse, non-focal pattern for no-tumor cases.}
\label{fig:gradcam}
\end{figure}

The glioma heatmap matches the visibly abnormal tissue in the upper part of the slice. The meningioma heatmap is even more focused. It concentrates on a well defined mass next to the skull, which matches how meningiomas usually appear, as they grow attached to the dura outside the brain tissue. For pituitary tumor the gland is small compared to the whole slice, but the heatmap still concentrates on the sellar region at the skull base, which is the correct anatomical location. For no-tumor cases there is no single hot spot. The activation is spread fairly evenly across both hemispheres, which is expected as there is no tumor for the model to focus on. So these results show that the network is focusing on relevant anatomy and not on unrelated patterns in the image. But the resolution of Grad-CAM is limited, because it comes from a heavily downsampled feature map, so it cannot show precise tumor boundaries. Also we did not measure the overlap between the heatmap and the lesion quantitatively, and we did not repeat this analysis for the other three backbones.

\subsection{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

Two findings together shape how we read the accuracy of this pipeline. First, the choice of backbone matters, but less than the choice of inference strategy. Switching from EfficientNetB0 to DenseNet121 or ResNet50 gains 0.6 percentage points on CNN+TTA accuracy, and it does so consistently enough to be statistically significant for both, at $p=0.031$ each with a clean sweep of all five folds. Adding TTA to a fixed backbone gains between 0.6 points for DenseNet121, whose raw CNN was already strong, and 2.9 points for MobileNetV2, whose raw CNN was the weakest of the four. TTA is significant for every backbone we tested, but the size of the gain depends a lot on how good the backbone already was without it. A practitioner with a fixed and already strong backbone in production would get comparable value from either change. A practitioner with a weaker or lighter backbone would get more value from adding TTA than from swapping the backbone. Second, the embedding ensemble is consistently competitive with CNN+TTA but not consistently better than it. Its advantage over the plain CNN is statistically robust for three of the four backbones but not for DenseNet121, whose already strong CNN leaves the fold-wise advantage of the ensemble too inconsistent in direction for five folds to separate from chance.

The confusion patterns are consistent across backbones. Meningioma varies a lot in appearance on imaging and it does not always have clear boundaries. This explains why it was the class most often confused with glioma and pituitary tumor in every configuration we tested. No-tumor has no lesion to be confused with, so it was the easiest class everywhere.


\section{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}
Several factors limit how much these results should be trusted. The dataset does not include a patient ID field, so cross-validation is done at the image level. Near-duplicate images could end up on both sides of a fold, especially as the dataset itself is a merge of several public sources. This is a form of subject-level leakage, and it is a common and often unaddressed source of inflated performance in medical imaging machine learning \cite{varoquaux2022leakage}. We do not have a patient-identified version of this same dataset, so we cannot directly measure how much of the reported accuracy is affected by this. A dataset with the same scale and class taxonomy but with recorded patient identifiers would let this question be tested as a controlled experiment instead of leaving it as an open concern.

The dataset snapshot we use has 7,200 images with exactly 1,800 per class. This differs from the 7,023-image composition with an imbalanced no-tumor class that is reported for the same Kaggle dataset identifier by some of the works we compare against in Section~\ref{sec:sota} (Section~\ref{sec:eda}). We cannot confirm whether the dataset owner updated the release between the time those studies downloaded it and the time we did, or whether there is some other difference in how the data was obtained. Either way, our benchmarking comparison should be read as using the same publicly available dataset identifier and not a byte-for-byte identical set of images. The exploratory analysis in Section~\ref{sec:eda} also shows that the images inside a class are not all from the same MRI acquisition plane, and that at least one no-tumor example is a CT scan and not an MRI slice. This qualifies the description of the dataset as uniformly T1-weighted MRI. Finally, the no-tumor class is visibly brighter on average and more consistently framed than the tumor classes (Section~\ref{sec:eda}). We cannot rule out that this contributes to the consistently strong performance on no-tumor, alongside or instead of its clinical distinctiveness.

The multi-backbone comparison in Section~\ref{sec:backbone_results} was run once per backbone. We did not repeat the Grad-CAM analysis for ResNet50, DenseNet121 or MobileNetV2, because of the extra GPU time each full backbone run needs. Section~\ref{sec:efficiency} shows that a single 5-fold backbone run costs 1.5-2.5 GPU-hours. The Grad-CAM attention patterns may differ by backbone, and we have no evidence either way. We also did not run a component-wise ablation to isolate the individual contributions of class weighting and the training-time augmentation policy, as both were part of the pipeline from the start for every backbone tested.

The Wilcoxon signed-rank tests in Section~\ref{sec:stats_results} are limited by the small number of folds. With $n=5$ the smallest attainable one-sided $p$-value is $1/32 \approx 0.031$. So a comparison can report significance at $\alpha=0.05$ only when one configuration wins every single fold. Anything less than a clean sweep, for example the 3 of 5 record of MobileNetV2 against EfficientNetB0 in Section~\ref{sec:stats_results}, cannot reach significance at this sample size, no matter how large the underlying effect is. These tests should be read as evidence about the consistency of direction across folds. They are not precise estimates of effect size and they are not a substitute for a larger independent replication.

A related limitation is run-to-run variance beyond what the fold-wise standard deviations capture. Two independent runs of the identical EfficientNetB0 configuration, with fixed random seed and identical code, gave 5-fold mean accuracies that agreed within 0.1 percentage points overall but differed by up to 0.4 points on individual folds (Section~\ref{sec:cv_protocol}). This fits with the known GPU-level non-determinism in some cuDNN kernels. So the fold-to-fold standard deviations reported in this paper probably understate the true uncertainty by a small amount, as they do not include this extra source of variance. We did not repeat this reproducibility check for the other three backbones.

Besides, this study does 2D slice classification and not full 3D volumetric analysis, which is a simplification compared to standard radiological practice. Finally, all the data comes from a limited set of source institutions and scanners, so it is unclear how well this pipeline would work on MRI acquired at other hospitals or on other equipment. These gaps motivate the future work in Section~\ref{sec:futurework}.


\section{Ethical Considerations}
\label{sec:ethics}
This study uses only a publicly available and de-identified MRI dataset (Section~\ref{sec:datasets}). It did not involve direct interaction with human participants and it did not require institutional ethical review or informed consent beyond what the original dataset publisher obtained. In spite of the accuracy levels reported here, any deployment of a model like this in a clinical or screening setting should be treated strictly as a decision-support aid for a qualified radiologist or clinician and not as a diagnostic replacement. Final diagnostic decisions should stay under clinical supervision and should be confirmed through standard protocols. The dataset used here comes from a limited set of source institutions and scanner types (Section~\ref{sec:limitations}). It also does not include demographic metadata such as age, sex or ethnicity, which would be needed for a fairness or subgroup-performance audit. So we cannot rule out uneven performance across patient subgroups that are not represented in the training data. We recommend such an audit, together with external validation on data from additional institutions, before any deployment beyond a research setting.


\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
In this work we evaluated a hybrid brain tumor classification pipeline across four backbone architectures under a single identical 5-fold cross-validation protocol, instead of reporting a single accuracy figure for a single configuration. The pipeline consists of two-stage backbone fine-tuning, class-weighted training, test-time augmentation and a Random Forest/XGBoost/SVM embedding ensemble. On a four-class dataset of 7,200 images the best configuration is ResNet50+TTA. We got 98.38\% $\pm$ 0.23\% mean accuracy across 5 folds, with DenseNet121 close behind. Both of them beat the original EfficientNetB0 backbone in every one of the five folds ($p=0.031$ each). The lightweight MobileNetV2 reached 97.96\%, which is itself ahead of the 97.75\% of EfficientNetB0, at 14-31\% lower training cost than the other three backbones and with the lowest inference latency of the four. An exact Wilcoxon signed-rank test confirmed that the improvement of TTA over the plain CNN softmax output is statistically significant for every backbone tested, and that the improvement of the embedding ensemble is significant for three of the four backbones. We present these results together with the limitations in Section~\ref{sec:limitations} as evidence that multi-backbone comparison and explicit statistical testing should be standard practice in this literature. We do not claim that any single configuration reported here is a definitive state-of-the-art result.


\section{Future Work}
\label{sec:futurework}
Several directions follow directly from the limitations above. Repeating the Grad-CAM analysis for ResNet50, DenseNet121 and MobileNetV2, and not only for EfficientNetB0, would show whether the attention patterns reported here are backbone-general or backbone-specific. Identifying or annotating patient identifiers for this dataset, or evaluating on an equivalent dataset that already records them, would let the leakage concern of Section~\ref{sec:limitations} be tested directly. A component-wise ablation that isolates class weighting and the augmentation policy, alongside the backbone and TTA/ensemble comparisons already reported here, would complete the picture of which pipeline components drive the gains. Multi-scale feature fusion or extra attention mechanisms in the backbone may help with boundary-ambiguous cases like meningioma, which caused the most errors in every configuration in this study. A CNN--Transformer hybrid or a newer state-space (Mamba-style) architecture is another backbone family that we did not explore here. For interpretability, a quantitative evaluation that measures the overlap between the Grad-CAM heatmap and an annotated lesion mask, on a dataset that provides one, would give stronger evidence than visual inspection alone. Finally, this pipeline has not been tested on MRI from institutions outside the source of this dataset, so external validation is a necessary next step before any of these configurations could be considered for clinical use.



